The DBAS22\_DocOnline folder contains all the documentation files. The MainDataExplained file lists and describes the variables recorded during the experimental phases. In the SummaryOfFiles document, you will find descriptions for all the files within the DBAS22\_DataOnline folder, and at the bottom, there is also a file tree that illustrates the file structure. The DBAS22FilesWorkflow document offers an overview of the workflow of experimental file creation during the experiment.

The DBAS22\_CodeOnline folder contains all the scripts to perform data analysis, listed and described in the files CodeExplanations and DependenciesRelations. The GuideInstall file contains information needed to run the Python code files.

The DBAS22\_CodeOnline folder also contains the DataPlayer Unity project. Instructions for running the project are provided in the DataPlayerGuide file and SupplementaryVideo2 (see Related identifiers section for more details). The folder DBAS22\_DataPlayer\_StandAloneApp contains the standalone version of the DataPlayer, which doesn't require any software installation.

The DBAS22\_VideoOnline folder contains all the videos.},
	language = {eng},
	urldate = {2025-04-01},
	publisher = {Zenodo},
	author = {Lento, Bianca and Segas, Effie and Leconte, Vincent and Doat, Emilie and Danion, Frederic and Péteri, Renaud and Benois-Pineau, Jenny and de Rugy, Aymar},
	month = may,
	year = {2024},
	keywords = {Arm motion, Gaze data, Human movement, Pick and place task, Prosthesis control, Virtual reality},
}

@article{lento_3d-arm-gaze_2024,
	title = {{3D}-{ARM}-{Gaze}: a public dataset of {3D} {Arm} {Reaching} {Movements} with {Gaze} information in virtual reality},
	volume = {11},
	copyright = {2024 The Author(s)},
	issn = {2052-4463},
	shorttitle = {{3D}-{ARM}-{Gaze}},
	url = {https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-024-03765-4},
	doi = {10.1038/s41597-024-03765-4},
	abstract = {3D-ARM-Gaze is a public dataset designed to provide natural arm movements together with visual and gaze information when reaching objects in a wide reachable space from a precisely controlled, comfortably seated posture. Participants were involved in picking and placing objects in various positions and orientations in a virtual environment, whereby a specific procedure maximized the workspace explored while ensuring a consistent seated posture by guiding participants to a predetermined neutral posture via visual feedback from the trunk and shoulders. These experimental settings enabled to capture natural arm movements with high median success rates ({\textgreater}98\% objects reached) and minimal compensatory movements. The dataset regroups more than 2.5 million samples recorded from 20 healthy participants performing 14 000 single pick-and-place movements (700 per participant). While initially designed to explore novel prosthesis control strategies based on natural eye-hand and arm coordination, this dataset will also be useful to researchers interested in core sensorimotor control, humanoid robotics, human-robot interactions, as well as for the development and testing of associated solutions in gaze-guided computer vision.},
	language = {en},
	number = {1},
	urldate = {2025-04-01},
	journal = {Scientific Data},
